tandard, um, in basic testing when it comes to, uh, immunoassay. So I think a lot of, a lot of terrific potential there, particularly as it relates to advances in, uh, in public health and population, uh, testing, um, and some of the work that's being done by, by public health authorities, um, to look for hotspots over time. Um, in terms of your question about Cytiva and, um, and, and, and work around, uh, therapies and vac- vaccines, I'd say both Pall and Cytiva are uniquely positioned to provide, you know, pretty critical inputs, meaning filtration and resins to both vaccine and therapeutic candidates. Um, right now we would estimate that they're greater than 150 therapeutic and vaccine candidates today. Um, and Pall and Cytiva are working with a majority of those in some capacity. And, uh, so obviously there'll be, there'll be winners and losers, but we think we have a, a number of, of exceptional positions there with, uh, with folks that are likely gonna be part of the future therapy, um, and or, um, vaccine, uh, answers. So I think a good, good spot to be in. Great. I appreciate it. And congrats again on the retirement. Thanks, Tycho. Your next question comes from the line of Vijay Kumar with Evercore ISI. Morning Vijay. Um, morning, guys and Tom, uh, congrats on a, on a well earned, uh, rest. Um, I think, uh, you know, maybe starting with the leadership, uh, question here, Tom, um, you know, you mentioned that Rainer, um, not, not, you know, he's known to the street, uh, but, uh, perhaps, uh, you know, not, not everyone knows him well, uh, and you did mention that he was the right person. Maybe contrast your leadership style versus, with Rainer's leadership style. Uh, what, what does Rainer, uh, bring here, um, you know, and what should investors look forward to under this transition? Sure, well, um, I'd start, uh, Vijay with the fact that, um, Rainer and I have worked together closely, um, for virtually all of his, his 10 years. Um, you know, I don't know if 