t just talking about the executed, but there's a component around procurement and, uh, and, uh, and I to be savings, uh, innovative value that is going independent. And when we did a 250 mi- million dollar customers in your funnel, it was on based on 2019 kind of, uh, uh, runway level, so to speak or spend levels. So we just wanna be prudent from, uh, from kind of going out there and saying that the 250 will increase. Uh, you know, once we're, we're ready, we'll definitely. And when we see as kind of more stability or noma- normalization in the markets, maybe we come back with that. But at this point in time, we're, we're accelerating what we can control. And we know that we can control that structural head countdown. And that's exactly what we executed. We know we can control a lot of the quick gains in procurement because commodities are lower and we are executing that, and we know we can control a lot of discretionary spend, and that's exactly what we also executed. So, we're very focused on kind of going through the list of things that we can really execute. Right. Appreciate the color. <inaudible>. Your next question comes from Nathan Jones from Stiefel. Your line is open. Morning, everyone? Morning Nathan. Hey, uh, you know, revenue down 8.6 X FX, margins up 120 basis points. So, clearly some very good control lag on what drove the very good decremental there. Uh, how do you say the decremental is going forward, and, and maybe any color you can give us on what you think the long term margin opportunities in that business. Hey, Nathan, this is, uh, uh, uh, you know, I'll, I'll say some very good cost controls, but also some very good momentum, that we had also from the medical, uh, business. If you remember last year when we talked about the medical business, we were seeing upwards of 200 basis points margin improvement, and the medical business finishing last year at roughly, you know, 30, 31%, uh, EBITDA margin. So, again, very good momentum from that business. And, and, and clearly as we saw some suppliers in the market, the team continued to execute those, uh, targets that they needed, they needed, needed to get done. You know, I think e- when, when, when, when, when you look at this business that, uh, I mean, has some very nice gross margins, and, uh, you know, the decremental, the base decrementals are typically, you know, 45 or so. I mean, pretty good job that the team data here in order to get that decremental down to the 15%. Uh, and I'll say, you know, from a long-term perspective, we'll, we'll definitely come, come back, uh, with, with, with giving some kind of medium to longterm perspective, I'll just do a quick comparison, then you can see that medical, we were able to, uh, you know, not, not only, you know, just a few years ago, that medical business wasn't the 25, 26% EBITDA margin. And we finished, you know, last year in the 31% EBITDA margin. And there's a just a lot of good commonalities between the medical and the Legacy, uh, you know, PFS on Aero business that are within this segment. Okay. Maybe just a one on receivables. Um, when you look through that, do you see any customer credit risk, any col- risk there? I guess it's particularly an upstream comment, uh, given the way, given the way that market's going, but anywhere else you see, you know, any potential issues in receivables, how are you going about managing customer credit? Those kinds of things? I mean, I would say not necessarily. They, I mean, I think it's r- it's one that we, we, we, we, we live by day by day. I mean, clearly the, uh, on the, uh, on the high-pressure solution, which is, as you just mentioned the most exposed. I mean, customers are still paying. Uh, they take longer to pay, but they still pay. Uh, you know, they, they, they also realize that, uh, you know, from an upstream perspective, that our business is critical and essential for when the market comes back up again. So, uh, we have been pretty strict in, uh, in many cases