st related to the last question, I guess. Um, you, you have, uh, taken some stakes in, in certain startups. Um, and I have seen that, uh, one of those, uh, Justo, uh, have been, uh, growing quite, quite a bit. Uh, any insights or things that you have learned on, on the delivery in, in, in these stakes that, that you can share? Yes. You know, we, we, we've been trying to support the, uh, ven- uh, with some pension money, uh, to support those suppliers, uh, that are very much in line with us. So, we'll help those, uh, to enhance our, our value proposition. And, and I, I don't know, uh, specifically the one you're talking about. Juan, well, might have a better idea of, of… But yes, we're working not only with technological suppliers, but also with, with, uh, normal suppliers, where we could help them, uh, to become larger. And in fact, we did that with a, um, a great supplier that we have with this, Caffenio, which is the one that supported all the coffee operations. And I think, with that knowledge, we could be of, of great, uh, uh, support to them. So, this pandemic, we also, um, uh, we, we, all the small, uh, uh, vendors, we are helping them to pay them very quickly, so they don't have this cash currency problem that this pandemic will cost to them. And, and, uh, so, that, that, that, those are the, the, yes, but our, our small-scale suppliers are very important. And the strategic ones, we want to invest with them so they can grow better and faster to become a major competitive advantage to us. Yeah. I think on the, on the, uh, Justo, or Justo, um, investment, I mean, certainly, we're, we're trying to, to help, uh, these, these new ventures through, through our FEMSA Ventures arm, uh, but also learn, uh, about, uh, you know, things that, that would help us in our own, uh, value proposition or, or the way that we operate. And certainly, uh, distribution, the use of, um, you know, dark, dark warehouses, or dark kitchens, dark stores, um, the handling of perishables, uh, these are all things that, that we would love to, to be better at and to know more about. And this, this investment, in particular, obviously, has, has, uh, tur- it turned out to be, uh, an interesting one. Um, I mean, the, the, the, the, the amounts involved are, are not huge and relative to the size of FEMSA. But certainly, it's a startup that is doing very well. And of course, uh, I know that you stay close to, to, to these companies and, and, and their, you know, the different rounds of financing and, and how quickly they're growing. And, and this is so far a very nice success story. Well, uh, thanks, uh, Eduardo and Juan. <unk> And we will go to Alvaro Garcia of P- BTG Pactual. Good morning, Eduardo, Juan, Eugenio. Uh, my questions, I have two questions. Sorry. I have two questions. One on Heineken. I was wondering if you could comment on whether or not your position on the stake on owning, you know, this, this 15% has changed or not, given the new tax agreement with the Mexican government. And two, uh, on M&A, is it fair to say you're in integration mode? It would seem to be a nice time to sort of take a breather and, and, and integrate a lot of the different, uh, assets you, you bought over the last couple of years. Um, those are my two questions. Thank you. Well, the Heineken, the Heineken shares, uh, yes, it's a temporary investment. We just have to try to, uh, a way to, uh, uh, deploy that capital in a better way. And, and, and, um, it, it is, it, it is as, as not as optimal as, as it was in the past, as you are referring. Uh, but it's, we just have to find the, the, the right time, uh, and, and the right path to, to, uh, deploy that capital and, and, and have that, um, um, use of those proceeds. Um, the, the second question, uh, I forgot. Uh, Juan, would, uh, uh, I, I, I forgot the second, the, the second point. <crosstalk> Are we ready to take a breather after a lot of activity on the M&A front? Um, and I think, I mean, I, I think that the answer to that is, is yes. Uh, for the most part- That's good. uh, you should expect us to, to start integrating, uh, capturing synergies and kind of, uh, delivering on the promise of, uh, of all of these investments. Um, I would, I would say there, there's probably one, one, uh, exception. Uh, and, you know, the, the Jan-San platform, uh, you know, a big part of the, of the attractiveness of that business involves, um, a growth through acquisitions and eventual integration. And, you know, it's hard to tell at this point whether there will be, uh, transactions kind of coming along, uh, in the coming months and quarters. But if they were, um, I think we would be interested in taking looks. Uh, but other than that, for the most part, and understanding that M&A is hard to predict, uh, generally speaking, the, the, our stance is, is one of, as you say, kind of sitting a little bit more on our hands, um, and digesting the, the, the number of bites that we took over the last 18 months. Yes. And Alvaro, eh, uh, um, uh, thinking about the fourth platform that we are looking, I think we already have it. So, we, we, the, the, uh, we don't think to open a new vector, uh, uh, a different vector for growth. We, the ones we have is, is already well, well established, the Proximity, Health, um, uh, and, and, um, uh, and now this Jan-San platform. I think we're already very much foc- we will be very much focused in, into the platform that we already have. Great. That's very clear. Thank you very much. And now we will take our next question from Sergio Matsumoto of Citi. Uh, yes. Hi. Good morning. Uh, thank you for taking my questions. Uh, I, I have, uh, uh, two questions. One is, I wanted to go back on the, uh, again, portfolio and the, the assortment question, uh, at OXXO. Uh, uh, you spoke about how you can, you know, change some of the assortments, uh, given the, the, the economic slowdown. But, uh, could you, uh, explain more on the assortment changes you can make, uh, in the face of the, the, the reduced mobility, uh, and, and especially over the long term, if this changes the, the consumer habits, uh, like, if, if, uh, the mobility stays low for a long time? That's the first question. And the second question is, in Brazil logistics, uh, which are the biggest industries that, that, uh, those businesses serve? Uh, Sergio, uh, I, I think in, in, we have better abilities and, and competencies to suit the assortment for an economic downturn. Um, lack of mobility is, it will be, uh, uh, different because, really, they're also designed to serve, uh, people that are on, on the go and are on the street. We have had, in the past, uh, the daily and replenishment category. And, and that is the, uh, groceries. We could not really bind the groceries. And also, it's really the one that the, the, the one that you are left out in your pantry. And, and really, that's really the occasion that we have, have been sharing. I think, in this pandemic, we've been able to, uh, to compel, uh, and the consumer that we have a, uh, uh, a good assortment of, of, of, for, for that, uh, pantry, uh, uh, um, uh, well, to find the product you are, that, that you are left out in, in your, in, in your pantry. And, and I think, by testing more and finding more, they are finding that we have be- uh, good prices. But I think that category, um, it will, um, if lack of mobility will be in place, we'll have to expand that category compared with the, uh, um, uh, uh, quench or, or, or some other categories that are more designed to serve people that are on the go. But I think that would take more, uh, the more, uh, time and, and effort than product to the economic downturn. Juan? Yes. No. I, I think, uh, uh, uh, I mean, on, on that front, uh, it, it, it will be interesting for all of us to, to see, um, whether, you know, the consumer begins to privilege Proximity even more, right, in terms of these, uh, traditionally kind of supermarket-type categories, and whether our assortment and our pricing, uh, is, is compelling enough, uh, which probably we will, we will expect that it will be, uh, for some of these cust- consumers to decide to not just, uh, buy the, you know, the cooking oil or the rice that they forgot or that they run out of, uh, during, you know, between trips to the supermarket, but that they will use OXXO as a more, uh, frequent, uh, source of this type of products. And then, we will make the necessary adjustments as, as we go along. Uh, I think on, and I mentioned earlier, the, you know, the, the comment about spirits as another, uh, potential, uh, category where we, uh, hopefully, uh, will become more kind of top of mind for the consumer in terms of how, how good an assortment, and, and how good our pricing is, uh, for, for that, uh, category. Um, I think you also touched on, on, the second part of your question, on, uh, the categories for Brazil logistics. Um, I think, generally, as we have been moving, uh, towards the LTL, the less-than-truckload business, uh, and the specialized warehousing, uh, certainly, pharma, uh, is an industry that is very well suited, um, for the, for the type of, uh, logistics that, that, that we do, uh, not just in the distribution part, but also on the, on the warehousing side. In fact, if you look at AGV, the, the company that we acquired a few months ago, uh, the, you know, their biggest, their biggest category, uh, actually has to do with, with health, uh, certainly, a big component of, of, uh, animal or veterinary health, but also human health. I think when you think about the, the, the equation or the relationship between the size of the products, uh, the weight, and the, and kind of the volume, and the cost, um, and, and the type of, uh, conditions that they require for the transportation and their storage, uh, it's an attractive industry. It's a profitable industry, and it's a very good fit with, with what we do. So, I would say you should expect us to continue to try to grow in, in that front. Obviously, there are others. There are CPG products and categories and companies, electronics companies. But, uh, certainly, the health, uh, you know, for both, uh, human and, and veterinary, uh, stand, stand out as, as, as, uh, probably one of the biggest, if not the biggest. Thank you. And next question will come from Carlos Laboy of HSBC. Good morning, everyone. Um, John and, uh, Constant- Hi, Carlos. Hi, Eduardo. John and Constantino spoke of a concentrate price readjustment yesterday. Um, uh, uh, as the controlling shareholder and with really important rights in your equity agreement with Coke, what role is FEMSA playing in this negotiation? And what can you tell us about the long-term clarity of profit split with the brand owner? Well- Are they, are they adequate for you at this point? Carlos, this agreement was signed, like, uh, four years ago. And yes, we want to have, we would love to have a more stable and certain long-term relationship model. And that, that is something that is important for us and, and, and, and important not only for the, uh, FEMSA's controlling shareholder. It's very important for management because the way we, we compensate the management. They have to have some certainty because, uh, uh, that is very important for the long run, and also for the, uh, uh, minority shareholders. Uh, the only, uh, the Coca-Cola company is, is, uh, uh, understand this. And, and I think it, it really is, is, um, we just have to be very creative and, and, and, and, and, and, and empathetic from both sides in the stand, but we are at the same ship. That is very important because, probably, in the, in the past, we were not as empathetic of, to understand the income statement of the Coca-Cola Company, and probably they were not as empathetic to understand our income statement. And I think with, through these, uh, conversations that we have had with them, we are very much aligned, and then they have a total understanding that we're exactly in the same boat. And the current economic circumstances and the current consumer, uh, uh, uh, behavior, we have to be very much aligned. So, we are trying to build as much aligned as possible, alignment as possible, and certainty, so we can invest and, and develop and tackle the consumer the best we can. And as I said, we love, and, and I think the main two objections have to be fully aligned, fully aligned, and the, uh, uh, build certainty and trust in the relationship for the long run. So, the tenure of the last agreement was a 10-year agreement. This one, you just got a negotiation four years ago that just, just, you just finished making your last payments on it. Do you know what the tenure of this agreement is going forward? Or are we just really negotiating a long-term agreement now? Uh, no. We, we, we have this established. And, and, and, uh, but it's, it's, um, um, something that they, they have the time, and, and, and, and we, we cannot disclose this to you, to you, uh, without them, uh, uh, being fully aware. But don't worry. We're very happy with it. And, and, and, uh, and in fact, we love to have this kind of agreement everywhere else. We have given the certainty for the long run and alignment. Alignment, certainty, and trust. That's helpful. Thank you. Yeah, I think, Car- Carlos, if I, if, if I could, um, you know, a few weeks ago in, in, in one, uh, of the, uh, group calls that, that you, you, you've, uh, helped us, uh, set up with, with investors, you, you asked me the question of, uh, you know, is it possible to have a, uh, a long-term relationship with, with Coke where, where both partners, uh, realize, uh, uh, uh, a reasonable spread of ROIC versus WACC. And, and my answer, uh, was, was, yes, yes, yes, we can, right? I mean, yes, it is possible. And I think the point I would make right now is that, this is definitely, uh, not at odds with what, uh, you know, what John and Constantino said yesterday. I mean, uh, the alignment that Eduardo is talking about, the expectation that there is a formula, uh, where both partners can make the, the right level of returns, uh, is what we are looking at. Thank you. And now we'll take a question from Ricardo Alves of Morgan Stanley. Good morning, gentlemen. Thanks for the call. Um, most of my questions have been answered. But, uh, if I may just insist a little bit more on the Jan-San asset. Appreciate your comments earlier. Now, perhaps a bit more of a strategic question. I mean, how, how do you, you know, how are you going to be unlocking value on this acquisition in the long run? I mean, Juan just mentioned the, the M&A potential. Uh, but, you know, uh, as you merge the two companies, you come up with the venture synergies and so forth, what's the plan here in terms of how it fits to the overall, um, FEMSA platform? Or perhaps it's just that the growth profile of these assets just improved a lot with the dynamic, and then, you know, M&A would really be the key driver for, for future cash generation. So, just a few thoughts there would be helpful. Um, and, and then, just a quick update on the JV in Brazil, uh, as well. Yeah. I, I think we have a, uh, uh, uh, very good platforms for the medium and long run. I think this pandemic has caused some, some, some, uh, uh, damages, but I think in the medium and long term, we are very optimistic. We have the Health Division where, and we have opportunities there for growth. We have the Proximity Division, and then we have, uh, uh, we're very optimistic of, of the, of the performance we're making in, in South America and the JV we made in, in, in, in Brazil. And also, with our partnerships that we have here in, in, in, in, in, in, in the United States, we're very pi- optimistic. We have, uh, two great platforms. We have the, uh, uh, the Jetro Restaurant Depot. Without doubt, uh, Jetro has been eating share part of the competitors. And at least a major competitive, uh, um, uh, uh, uh, strength that they have, and we're very happy with that. And on the other hand