ion something about an update on the, on the JV in Brazil. Um, you know, obviously, the, the pandemic, uh, throws a little bit of sand into the gears, uh, in terms of the, the, the pace of openings, uh, down there. But quite frankly, um, we are almost, uh, on track, uh, in terms of, uh, openings for both the first, uh, new stores on the Select side, on the, on the gas stations of Raizen, uh, but also on the, on the standalone OXXO side. So, we, we do aspire to start opening stores, uh, OXXO stores, uh, towards the end of the year. Uh, in, in Brazil, we're already, uh, making progress in terms of, uh, setting up our first distribution center down there as well. Uh, so, things are going, uh, very much according to plan. And like I said, I think the pandemic has not, uh, forced the team down in, in, in Brazil to depart very much from the original, uh, plan. Obviously, this is, uh, we've said before, this is not going to be a piece of cake. Uh, you know, figuring out the right val- value proposition for, for the Brazilian OXXO is, is going to take a little bit of time. Uh, but the, the size of the opportunity and, and certainly, the, the partnership that we have, uh, make us very, very optimistic that, uh, this will eventually be an important part of the portfolio. And I think the, the partnership that we have with, with Cosan, and, and, and, uh, I think if, uh, if we found, uh, that the Proximity Division really, uh, entire commercial found the right way to go into Brazil. They are very happy. They are learning a lot. In fact, they are learning, uh, through, uh, um, and, and they are going to do, adopt some of the Select operations to this new scheme. And I think there is a, uh, a lot of, uh, we see a lot of growth, uh, and, and potential to adjust the value proposition of the Select stores and the OXXO stores in the field. And I think that will be very, um, uh, uh, uh, but it will take time. It will take time, but I think we're very optimistic. Got it. Thank you so much. Now, we go back to a follow-up from Gustavo Oliveira of UBS. Uh, thank you for taking my followup question. You know, if I may, um, yesterday, at Walmex, uh, conference call, they may show, uh, uh, some risks, uh, with respect to the introduction of the new labeling, uh, regulation rules that will start already in October. Uh, could you please, uh, help us to understand what could be the impact of those, uh, new labeling rules, uh, in your, in your OXXO business, perhaps during the transition time now, uh, as, as, as it's implemented in October? And perhaps in, in your ability of, of, or on your suppliers' ability to push for, for sales growth? We, we think that the, uh, sometimes, too much information for the consumer, and, and, and, and, and it seems like, uh, what I've been learning, I think it's very important to inform the consumer, so the consumer can do the better decision for the, uh, their, their, uh, I don't know, for their health and, and, and then nutrition and everything. Sometimes, if the, if the, if the, uh, labeling goes a bit too far, the consumer might be blocking some of, some of, uh, so, so much information that he doesn't want to, uh, uh, uh, uh, he, he's just, uh, overloaded by this, uh, uh, uh, labeling. We don't know really where we would stand. And, and I think, but, but, but, currently, we have been working very closely with, uh, with the, with the, our suppliers. And I think we'll be very, in, in a very good position to adapt to this new, uh, environment. We don't see major derails, uh, uh, ha- happening. Um, I don't know. You want to add to, uh, to this one. I mean, uh, just, just the point that, I think, on, on, on beverages, certainly on the Coke FEMSA side, generally, um, the consumer has been well informed for many years now about, uh, you know, calorie count and that sort of thing. And then, the, the industry has been evolving for a while, increasing the percentage of the, of the portfolio that has reduced, uh, or no calories at all. Um, and so, I think the, you know, the challenges from the, from the relabeling involve more, you know, the costs and the, kind of the, the operating, uh, conundrums of, you know, changing labels or adjusting your packaging, that sort of thing, but not so much in terms of the incremental information that the consumer will receive. There are, there are other categories where, uh, maybe the, the consumer has not been as, as, as exposed to nutritional information. Um, and I think, for those, there, there, there will be a period of adaptation. Uh, but to Eduardo's point, uh, you know, I think also is, is, is, it's very well positioned, and the, the, the flexibility and the, the, the level of, uh, dialogue and cooperation with suppliers, uh, leads, I think, to, to what should be a relatively smooth transition when this eventually becomes a, uh, uh, a fact. Uh, during this transition, uh, Juan and Eduardo, uh, do you foresee, uh, um, the risk of a, uh, a more aggressive markdown, just to get rid of the inventory that is with the old labeling and befo- or, or suppliers are going to, uh, assume the cost, um, you know, of, of that operation? I think that could impact your, your 4Q results or perhaps part of your 3Q results. No, but I, I, I think if we, we, uh, I ne- but I never, uh, the turnover of inventory in, in, in, in OXXO goes very fast. And if there will be some opportunities there, we will, then we'll continue to make some more money. Uh, uh, yes, I, I, I think it'll be, yeah, probably, uh, uh, an alternative. I don't know if Juan… No, I, I agree with you that, uh, our inventory levels and, uh, you know, tend to be small and, and there, the turnover is just as fast. And I'm sure we will work with suppliers and come up, come up with promotional activity as required. Um, and I don't think it's something that is, that is causing us to lose a lot of sleep at this point. Thank you very much for the clarification. Thank you. Thanks, Gustavo. Thank you. And ladies and 