, deviations from that more when you get down to specific assets that may drive it to- towards five or less, or the other way for lower quality assets. Thank you. Our next question will come from Vikram Malhotra with Morgan Stanley. Please go ahead. Uh, thanks for taking the question. Um, this first one, you know, you had a nice, uh, improvement on the first day out, um, and you highlighted where you think it's going to kind of end up towards your end. I'm just wondering how sustainable that level is. Is there any CapEx kind of bump we should expect next year? Or do you think this level is sort of sustainable going forward? Vikram, obviously you, Chris I'll touch on it, maybe you jumped in, but, um, remember we're all, uh, social distancing. So we're, we aren't as easily able to, uh, <laugh> not talk over each other, which I apologize, Chris, but I, I would say on the dividend, you're exactly right. Vikram. Uh, Chris mentioned low 90s for this year, and certainly we're trying to steadily improve that. So we do hope to maintain that level if not improve it each year. Um, but you know, as Chris also talked about, we've got a lot of leasing, uh, strengthening as we go into the later part of this year and into next year. So we do expect that what probably benefited us, frankly, in the second quarter, and maybe, uh, even in the third would be a little less spending on TI, uh, because of the slowdown in leasing. We see that probably picking right back up later in the year and into next year. So, you know, it's, it's a little early to tell, uh, exactly where we'll be for 21, but we like the direction generally where we're headed. And, uh, we'll, we'll certainly have a better sense of that as we start putting our plans together for 21 later this year. Okay. Great. Um, and then- Todd is-… Sorry go ahead. I think Todd's right. I was, only thing I would add to that is just the, the idea that where we are terms of our capital, other than TI, I, I don't see any, any major shifts