environment, so it does make sense, but, uh, I'm guessing pro forma leverage with forward equity and Gail and Mercy, like those are the four times those directly come along. So kind of curious what, what you're, you know, teed up for? Yeah, I would say on the, on the pipeline, Jordan, um, you know, our team is, is, is always building and, and adding to our pipeline and we, we take the approach of, you know, I think if you look at what we've closed on this year, about 80% of that is what we would consider, you know, all for lightly marketed deals. So our team is out there constantly developing those relationships. We've identified buildings that we want to own, and, you know, we have dialogue with the sellers or, or, or brokers in those markets that, that, uh, we, we trust and, and, you know, are constantly trying to, to get those sellers to the table. So we, we think that behind what we've laid out here, certainly there's, there's more opportunity that we're working on, and I think that's where we see the growth in the pipeline, uh, coming and setting us up well for, for 202. So, um, I think that's why we, we have the confidence that we do because we see the dialogue, uh, that our team is having with these, these sellers that are, that are sort of behind what we've laid out here. And Jordan, I, I would add to that. I do think, I mean, I think maybe what's in your question too, is sort of, you know, are we preparing for something bigger? I think it's more about, I mean, in some ways, yes, but it's not a complete change of stripes for us. I think it's very much sticking to what Rob just described, really going after and targeting what we want rather than, you know, waiting on the marketed deals. That said, of course, we, we look at the marketed deals and if they measure up, we certainly go after them. And it's not, you know, we, we certainly are encouraged that, um, you know, we would like to, you know, go after some, some deals, if we can, in this situation where we, we seem, you know, to be able to, as you said, have the capital, the resources, but you know, again, we're gonna probably stick to you, as you, you would know us to do, stick to the quality side of things. Um, but we're encouraged by, you know, the productivity and the effectiveness that Rob described of sustaining a higher level of acquisition. I think that's the key and really dri… you know, recognizing the importance of, of prudent growth or external growth in addition to our internal growth to kind of work that algorithm of FFO growth per share. Is a mix. And I know, I think it was, you just, you just spoke to a question of a similar thing, but is a mixed kind of skew, continue to skew toward the adjacent type assets, you think? I certainly think we will, we will skew towards on an adjacent, um, versus off, but, you know, again, we're, we're okay with off. I think Rob touched on that. We are okay with that. I would say, you know, today we sit closer to almost 90% on an adjacent. Um, we have done a fair bit of adjacent, um, as you kind of point out and, and sometimes, you know, what you find is those are the ones that, you know, you can go in and target and get, um, um, you know, really get those assets more regularly. And, you know, the on-campus, it's tough because you're waiting for those hospitals to let that go typically. So, uh, we really liked the dynamic that of on an, on an adjacent and, and I could see, you know, the off fluctuating, I think we, we maybe last year hit around 25%, um, you know, off. So we're okay with that. It's still gonna skew that way towards, on an, on an adjacent though. Okay. And then lastly, like, um, maybe for Chris on the cap rates. Um, I- I've got the cash Cap rates. I'm kind of curious, you mentioned the escalators on Mercy. Um, can you give me the gap cap rate on that at disposition? And then, uh, I'm just curious what the escalators and gap cap look on the acquisition as well? Yeah. On, um, on Mercy, the, the gap and cash were pretty close to the same, because we're about at the midpoint of the, of the lease term. Um, so they're, they're kind of right on top of each other. Um, in terms of the, the new acquisitions that we're, that we're looking at, the escalators, as I mentioned, are pretty similar to what our existing portfolio is, you know, that two eight to two nine range, um, in terms of escalators and, uh, you know, it, it varies on the impact of the gap, uh, based off of the term that is inside of our acquisitions, but, but in general, we typically say the gap adds about 25 to 50 basis points, um, uh, to the, to the cap rate on a gap basis, uh, in our acquisitions. Okay. Thanks for clarifying. Our next question will come from Lucas, Heart- Heartrich with Green Street Advisors. Please go ahead. Thanks everyone for your time this morning. Uh, this is John on for Lucas. Uh, just a quick one from me. Uh, just looking at the improvements in your expectations for the cash releasing spreads on the multi-tenant side, uh, you know, what's behind that optimism? Uh, I'll say the USA trends are pretty favorable. And just wondering if there's actually some opportunity for some outside there as well. Uh, thank you very much. Yeah. If you really look at the change in our guidance on, um, on the cash leasing spreads, we did change it this quarter compared to last, but that really was just kind of bringing it back more in line with what we had seen pre- COVID. Um, last quarter we, we were taking, we didn't wanna be overly aggressive, um, you know, not having, uh, a lot of, of leasing data, uh, in the midst of, of what was going on. And so we had taken, um, had taken down our expectations, but as, uh, as we, uh, worked releases during the quarter and continued our discussions, um, you know, things have, have really shown, uh, um, great, um, you know, resilience. And so, uh, our expectations is that our cash leasing spreads we'll be able to kind of maintain what we had been seeing previously, um, which longterm we say, we, we expect, uh, three to four. Uh, and, and so we're, we're, uh, so far we're seeing that, uh, those types of metrics are, are holding up well. And our next question will come from Rich Anderson with SMBC. Please go ahead. Thanks everybody. Good morning. Um, so one thing we are not talking about is, um, the risk going forward of reinfection and, you know, certainly in many of the states that you traffic and we're, we're seeing some uptick, but no, no talk of sort of a, a reversal of, you know, elective surgery, uh, shutdowns and whatnot. Um, can you, can you discuss that a little bit about how, how you're seeing that play out? I mean, even though the availability or, or states are allowing for, uh, elective surgeries, are people really jumping in and, and doing it, and is there a risk that, you know, a month or so from now, we could be having another conversation about shutting it down? Rich, I, it's a very good question. And I think we all sort of have that unease, you know, going into the fall, I think collectively that, you know, things can change and, you know, we have the flu season coming and, and things get worse typically in the winter. So it's a very good question. I do think, you know, we don't wanna be glib about the optimism because, you know, it's, it's a long road here and, um, you know, maybe the toughest time ahead of us. The good news is I think is, I think Bethany pointed out and I pointed out and we've all seen, there's just been a lot of improvement on treatments and how to deal with this. And so I think we, we all feared, obviously in the front end that hospitals would just be totally overwhelmed, and obviously that was tested in a couple of places, including New York. And, and I think the good news is we came out on the better end of what we all feared. And so, you know, you're seeing it, as you said, in hotspots, you know, Houston went through it and you're seeing different places. Um, and, and the good news is they, they seem to be handling it and it's not creating these long lengthy shutdowns. You know, you did see in Texas the governor trying to push, you know, some, some again, you know, policies there, but it really actually was a bit of a carve out for ASCs, you know, in some of the outpatient surgeries, it was more about protecting that inpatient capacity. So I do think you're gonna see a little bit of that. We do have to brace for that. Um, in terms of hotspots, I don't think it's, you know, nationwide shut it all down the way it was early on. It's gonna be much more targeted. And, you know, I'm, I'm no expert any more than anybody on the phone, but as we watch this move around the country, you know, it's kind of, it's the hotspot, it's these, you know, fires that kind of come up and then burn out. And I hope that continues to, you know, you know, be quelled, you know, and, and contained. But, um, I, I think it's very encouraging what we're seeing. And even though we might, you know, experience some, some issues, I think we're encouraged. I mean, the public companies that report their surgery numbers have been really incredible. Um, so I don't think there's a big hesitation to come back and do it. It'll just be a matter of, you know, the public health officials continue to sort of treat this in a targeted way rather than a blanket way. And, and we think that will be the case. Okay. Um, my second question, uh, and last question is what, what is your perspective of how the hospital industry, um, looks like after this is all done? I mean, would you say the hospital in business, not the medical office business per se, but the hospital business is weakened by all this? Um, in the sense that, you know, we kind of went in over hospitals, uh, to begin with and, and, and maybe this fast tracks some consolidation or closings that probably, or maybe would've just happened eventually. Uh, I'm just wondering if, if you think sort of picking your, your, your, um, your relationships becomes even more important in the aftermath because hos, the hospital industry overall is, is probably a notch weaker because of everything that happened? Yeah. I think it's, it's fair, a fair assessment and good commentary. We, we were having a discussion with our board earlier this week about that with several health system leaders there on our board. And, and, and I think it's what you said. You're gonna… it's, it's a classic, you know, problem that these crises just like the financial crisis caused. The strong are gonna get stronger, the weak are gonna get weaker and there's different dynamics to that, but in the hospital, well, that's very true. And, you know, you're seeing an HCA do extremely well, you know, coming through this and I think they, they do a good job of figuring it out. And, and there's a lot of not-for-profit systems that are leaders in their markets. I do think the strength and, and scale matters, um, larger systems versus small independent system, obviously, you know, more, more urban, uh, versus in suburban versus rural is gonna be a huge trend. I know that, that the trend of moving into the cities everybody's questioning a little bit, but I still think those strong, um, you know, population growth centers are gonna be where you're gonna see the success. And so I do think you are correct that picking the right partners will matter more. Being a sharp shooter will, will continue to be critical for success. And I think that's something we've been honing ever since we sort of found that trend to be TRUE from the financial crisis even before. So it, it's true. I, I do think you're right. Hospital's probably taken, you know, they've clearly taken a hit and were helped a lot by the Cares Act, um, and CMS in general. But I do think we are encouraged that the strong ones, I mean, this is a demand need based business. You know, this isn't just, uh, you know, selling, uh, luxury items. It's, it's very much need-based. And so there's a huge need for it inpa… because I made my comments or remark that, I mean, hospitals are proving that they're so critical. And I think the acuity level of hospitals is only gonna continue to climb, and you're gonna see acuity level with outpatient climb both on and off campus. It's a continuum. And I think, um, you know, all those innovations will continue and, uh, it will be more, a little bit more of a winner versus loser at game. And, and I think we're, we're pretty well-positioned to, to follow that trend. And, and related to that response. Thanks, Todd. That was great. Um, do you think the long awaited monetization of hospital and medical office will, will be, you know, more of a realistic outcome of this even for stronger hospitals that maybe are now taking on, you know, more market share, need more technological advances and so on. Is, is that something that we can finally say? <laugh> Well, not, not, not to get religious, but it's like testing our faith, right? In this theory. <laugh> And it's just, we gotta keep the hope alive, right? Um, we just haven't seen it is the real answer. Um, it has all the makings to suggest that would be possibly true. Um, we have a very contradicting data point here with the Mercy transaction that we just did. A little bit of a specific situation where they had a propensity, you know, to, to want to own their assets. Uh, so a little different, but we've heard of a few other anecdotes just recently of some other hospitals doing similar things. So, uh, you know, hospital systems think strategically very differently. They don't focus, I mean, as they should. They focus on providing care and, and, and all the things they need to do to expand their business and succeed. They're not sitting around thinking about cap rates for MOBs, but I think there's gonna be, there are gonna be some more of those. It may be a little bit more from your stressed systems. And so then weeding through that back to your earlier question, figuring out is it worth it? Is it 