more free rent or you been more recently? Chris, do you wanna touch on that? Yeah. I mean, obviously free rent will go into it, if, if, if you have a larger percent, a percentage of it versus what you had before, but I'll say generally, no, we're not adding a lot of free rent. Um, you know, we really don't have much, if any, inside of our renewals, if we're, if we're doing it at all, it, it typically is inside of, of new leasing. Um, and so when you have that, uh, with the new leasing, it just kind of creates a little bit of a delay before you see the, the, the revenue impact from that absorption. Um, but, uh, but overall, no, we've, we've not seen, um, a marked expansion of, of free rent. Okay. And then, uh, maybe I'll ask Nick Joseph's question a little differently. Um, I think you mentioned $150 million of acquisitions you had under contract and now you've closed $83 million post quarter. Um, are you still on track to close that remaining 70 million or so, or have any of those deals fallen apart? Uh, yes. We're still on track to, to close those. Um, I think we've got, um, I think I mentioned we have 163 million, um, that's either under contract or, or LOI, so we've, we've actually in- increased that under contract amounts since, since the last time. And, and I think Rob too, you said, and we, we expect that we will close those two categories under contract NOI by year end, which is what will- Yes. help us get those Mercy proceeds reinvested by year end. Right. And the four adjacent, uh, acquisitions, uh, you, you mentioned are just A-rated health systems. Are those health systems at all tenants in the buildings required? Um, they have, I would say that there are, have, certainly have physicians that are, you know, utilizing the hospital, um, next door. Um, I think there's one where the hospital, um, is, is not a tenant, but, but all the others are, are tenant by the hospital. Thank you. Thanks, John. Our next question will come from Todd Stender with Wells Fargo. Please go ahead. Thanks. Uh, probably in order, just to stick with that last theme about the, the assets under a contract and maybe this is for Rob. Um, can you speak to the condition of those buildings? I know historically you guys are prepared to put some redevelopment dollars into them. Um, but in the context that maybe Todd, what you were speaking to, maybe TIs come down, maybe just getting some broader thoughts about how you guys are allocating capital. Thanks. Yeah, I would say that, um, the assets that were on a contract to, to purchase an ROI. I mean, they're all in, in good condition. I mean, we're not planning to invest a significant amount of capital, um, into any of them as an, as a redevelopment play. If that's, if that's answering your question. It is. Thank you. And, and maybe for Todd, um, did I hear that right? Maybe expectations of lower TIs coming down the road? I think that's a 2020 phenomenon. We're not necessarily suggesting that, um, looking into 2. We actually think it should rebound to sort of historical levels in relationships. Um, and, and all that is, is, is what we've heard some others say too. We had slower tours and, and new leasing activity with new perspective, met new tenants that create absorption, which is where you have higher TI spend, uh, in, in the second quarter, you know, especially April and even a little in May. And therefore you get this lag effect of when those tenants would normally take occupancy and that's probably a third and fourth quarter phenomenon. So it's really a back half issue and even a little bit, you saw it in the second quarter. So, um, you know, that's all we're saying. And then back to sort of normalize levels in 2. Okay. I would clarify that too, of, you know, tha- that's a little bit on the perspective leasing front, what Todd's talking about of, of… And we don't know for sure- Yeah. what, we've kind of run in different scenarios. And so we're looking at one scenario that, that, yes, we could see a little bit less absorption here in the next couple of quarters and then rebounding, um, which could have an impact. But then also if you look at it in terms of total spending, you start looking at fad right now, we are running, um, at the low end and frankly, a little below the low end of our guidance range, uh, as it relates to second gen TI. Um, and so there's just timing that is always related to that. Uh, so that may, you know, pick up, certainly we think we'll be in the range back to, you know, potentially near the midpoint of the range. Um, and so that's part of what, what is playing into the guidance as, as it relates to, uh, defad. In my prepared remarks, I've talked about, you know, right now we are, are running significantly below, um, where we are on a, on a trailing 12 month basis. But, um, you know, we do think that that can pick up in the later half of the year and, and what drives our, our view that, uh, our fad payout ratio will, will probably end up the year down in the, in the low 90's, uh, not in the low 80's that you saw this quarter. So I just wanted to, to clarify that point. That's helpful because that's where I was going u- ultimately with this is seeing that fad payout ratio declined and, and in the, in the spirit of seeing maybe some dividend growth at some point, um, but maybe it doesn't shake out that low. Okay. That's helpful. All right. That's it for me. Thank you. Thanks Todd. The next question will come from Tayo Okuseya with Mizuho. Please go ahead. Oh, yes. Good morning, everyone. Uh, congrats on the transaction. It makes perfect sense to me. Uh, my focus is really on the outlook for the MOBs. I, I think you mentioned in your comments earlier on that, uh, you know, hospitals in general and associate level they seem to be doing well when we kind of, you know, think about HCA results and some of the other hospital operators, but a lot of that is also kind of due to the massive amount of grants they've kind of received over this period as well. Uh, could you just kind of talk about how you're thinking about kind of in a world where there are no grants or in a world where, you know, where that source of government funding is, is decreased, maybe in the next, you know, act, that, uh, that Congress enacts. You know, what do you kind of see happens in that world? I mean, specifically, could we start to see deferrals going up again or, or, you know, would love for you to, to just kind of expand on that? Well, it's, you know, it's, it's all speculation obviously Tayo, but it's, it's interesting. Um, you know, I think we saw the most extreme thing, hopefully that we'll ever see, uh, in our lifetimes, right? But maybe not. Uh- Amen to that. Right, right. So, you know, April was just absolutely extreme and, and clearly the government steps in and we just hope, you know, we don't see that again, but I think we've, as I mentioned, we've learned a lot and I think we learned that hospitals, uh, you know, good or bad, you know, hospitals and physicians, they really make a lot of money on the elective, uh, procedure, side in surgeries. And when that was shut down, I mean, you're cutting off the most profitable segment of the business and, you know, healthcare, it, it's, it's a little cold to say that because obviously they're only doing, they're, they're only supposed to be doing what's necessary, but it is necessary. There's a ton of that need. And so when you cut off that lifeblood, if you will, uh, it really hurts the system. So I think now if you go back to the discussions earlier where it's more of a targeted approach and maybe, you know, for a short period in Houston, they, they asked, you know, for some elective procedures to be, to be, you know, reduced and, and not even in all AFCs, but in hospitals. So I think you're gonna see a more targeted approach, um, rather than the sort of absolute, you know, month long or more shutdowns. So it should… Therefore, it shouldn't be quite as financially difficult. Um, you know, is it gonna be tough? Yeah. Just like school. Schools for a lot of us, you know, with kids and it's gonna be tough, you know, as soon as they shut down the school for a week two or something, or, or even if you're lucky if they're open. So there's gonna be ebbs and flows, as I mentioned in my remarks, but I think we're much better shape today than we were when we knew very little in March. So I don't think it's as dire as, you know, we could all fear, but it's, you know, it's never, David Emory used to always say, it's never as good or as bad as it may seem. And that's probably very appropriate here. It, it, it will be not easy, but it, it, I don't think it's gonna be as bad as it was. And, and I, it's not changing our outlook about hospitals and on-campus MOBs. Um, we'll watch that just like everybody. But I think in my own experience, you know, personally, if you've ever dealt with a family member or, or a friend or loved one, whatever it might be, who's had a serious illness, you don't think twice about going to the hospital or going to that, you know, high acuity specialists that might be near the hospital. When it gets real, this, that's what this is about. You know, the light touch stuff, and it's really cool that there's innovations that can do things, you know, in, in lower costs, you know, off-campus settings and that's fine. That's a very real thing. And, and not to say that trend is not real, but, you know, we, we think the dynamics of real estate and demand, supply and demand are best where it's that high acuity sort of, of nucleus, if you will, around the hospital and, and as Rich asked, you know, we gotta watch that nucleus, make sure it's healthy, the hospitals that we're working with. So that, that's our focus. That's helpful. Thank you. Thanks Tayo.", "source": "earnings21", "duration_s": 3595.01}
