ve any plans to enter into any new joint ventures at this time. But it was possible if the right opportunity came along that would entertain that, but for now, uh, that part of the business is really winding down. Okay great. And then, um, if I could just ask one more on the new debt you guys just raised, um, is the plan to pay off the 2022 short-term, or both are already <inaudible>. Yeah it's already done. <inaudible>. Yeah, we effectuated that, uh, last week, we closed on the new bonds, I believe it was on the 28th of October and all the, the previous 22 notes have been extinguished, um, and, uh, so that's done. And then we used some of our cash on hand, um, to pay off the balance because we raised 250 million in new notes and we had about roughly 292 million of principle on the old notes, so. Okay, great, I'll get back in the queue, good job. Sure thanks Alex. Thank you. Our next question comes from line of Jason <inaudible>, with a private investor. Please proceed with your question. Thank you for taking my question guys and congratulations on the strong quarter. Uh, my question will only just kind of pertain to the priorities for capital deployment going forward. Uh, you mentioned how you successfully leveraged the balance sheet and extended all maturities, um, and the outlook for the business seems exceptionally strong, uh, when you guys are significantly generating cash flow, uh, but with just stock trading at half of book-value, well basically half a tangible book-value, it's hard to really imagine there's a better use the cash right now, or the part where you use the cash right now then ploughing back into the stock, so really, what are your thoughts around potential share repurchasing and really how can we focus and think about priorities with capital deployment going forward? That's a good question Jason thank you. Um, yeah, you know we've, we've looked at all aspects of use of our capital and we've been very focused, as you've mentioned, on really deleveraging the business and refinancing our bonds and pushing out the revolver extension, which was a, was a big important thing that we, we'd completed on Friday, um, so it really puts us in a position now where we can reinvest in the business. You know obviously we did buy quite a few shares in the front half of the year when we saw the stock price depress very significantly and I agree with you, that trading at half, half of multiple book-value isn't, uh, where we'd like to be. Um, but on the, on the other hand, we do need to, uh, reinvest in our business a little bit and we will evaluate really all aspects and where to allocate the capital going forward. We do have a little bit left on our authorization from our board, I think there's about 1.7 million left authorized currently, um, but again we've been on pause on the land act side for, for a little bit now and, um, we will sort of layer some of that in going forward, um, again. We do need to sort of balance our capital location and it's a fair question, but we, we do need to reinvest in the business too, so we'll continue to evaluate on all funds moving forward. Um, John do you also want to, um, address the idea of where we are with leverage, where we came from and where you see us moving to the future? Yeah, I mean I think our net debt did come down significantly at 45.1% at the end of the quarter, um, you know its down almost 1,000 bases points from where we were a year ago, um, we will, as I mentioned in my prepared remarks, we will have a charge in the refinancing of the, the old bonds, which will come through in Q-4, um, but moving forward, we're probably running the company with the idea of between 45 and 50% on a net debt-to-cap bases moving forward. Any other questions? Not from me thank you and congratulations again and good luck. Thank you, thanks Jason. Thank you. Our next question is a follow up from the line Alex <inaudible> from Housing Research Center. Please proceed with your question. Yeah, thanks for taking my follow up. Um, so I noticed in Arizona you guys managed to open several communities this quarter, um, just wondering if there's anymore in the pipeline going into next year, or is this roughly gonna be the community <inaudible> through, through next year? Yeah Alex this is Leonard, you, you're right, um, you know the last five months we've opened seven communities in three different locations and obviously we are very excited about the early results, where we're seeing strong absorption and strong margins, so again, uh, we're really excited about our team there on the ground. We have one community slated to open in the first half of next year and, um, that's, you know at this point we have other things that we're currently working on, but that's primarily, uh, the one that we can talk about today. Yeah and it looks like we, we're, we're at eight communities now in the Q-3, I think one of them probably drops off in Q-4 and then we'll probably run like seven community range plus or minus for the year. Right. Depending on how quickly, uh, we sell through some of these communities. That's what we're expected for the bulk of next year, but it may tapper a little bit of Q-4 of next year until we reload the two communities we're working on now. Okay, great. Um, and then as far as gross margins, I think you gave us a range for next year, I mean for next quarter, which is a little bit downwards from this quarter. Is that mainly because of the impact of lumber or is there something else there? It, it really has to do with some of the close up communities we have on the luxury condominium side. The good news is we're selling three of those, the down side is that the margins are a little bit weaker in those communities with, on our higher average selling price Alex, so I think once those all get disposed of and delivered in Q-4, I think our margins, uh, look positive moving forward and again I think 13 / 8 to 14 / 2 we felt pretty good about that, but again part