ution. Our natural gas distribution segment lost four cents per share in the third quarter of 2020, compared with a loss of five cents per share in the third quarter of, uh, last year. Improved results were due to higher revenues. I should note that, uh, because we didn't close on, uh, our acquisition of Columbia Gas of Massachusetts assets until October ninth, the transaction had no impact, uh, on this, the gas segment, the segment, uh, during the quarter. Uh, each quarter this year, uh, we've booked acquisition related costs at the parent. It has segregated them for, uh, increased transparency. Beginning in the fourth quarter of this year, ongoing results of our new gas franchise, which is named Eversource Gas Company of Massachusetts, will be reflected in the natural gas segment. Integration related costs, however, will continue to be recorded separately at the parent, and excluded from our recurring GAAP, uh, earnings. Our water distribution segment earned seven cents per share in the third quarter of 2020, compared with earnings of six cents per share in the third quarter of 2019. Improved results, um, were due to a three, three and a half million, uh, dollar after tax gains on the sale of our Hingham, Massachusetts <inaudible> facilities, uh, to the town. Eversource parent earned three cents per share in the third quarter of 2020, excluding the Columbia Gas of Massachusetts asset acquisition costs, uh, equal to our earnings in the third quarter of, of last year. As you probably noted in our earnings release and can see on slide three, we are reaffirming our 2020 earnings per share guidance of $3.60 to $3.70, and that is excluded the non-recurring costs related to the purchase of Columbia Gas of Massachusetts assets. We are also reaffirming our long term EPS growth rate of five to seven percent from our core regulated business through the year 2024. We continue to be, uh, to expect to be somewhere around the middle of that range, uh, largely due to the investment we need to make on behalf of our customers as we've outlined to you, uh, earlier in the year. As a reminder, uh, while we fully expect the Columbia Gas assets to be <inaudible> to our earnings per share starting immediately, uh, in 2021, we have not yet updated our long term financial outlook to reflect the acquisition of Columbia Gas, uh, assets in our, our capital, our cap ex or our earnings growth. In addition, as we've disclosed previously, earnings from offshore wind would be incremental to our core, uh, business growth. We'll provide a comprehensive update of our regulated capital investment forecast, uh, adding in, uh, Eversource Gas Company of Massachusetts projections, and provide an update of our offshore wind partnership, uh, during our year end call in late February. From the third quarter results I'll turn to slide four and our experience restoring power after tropical storm Isaias ravaged Connecticut on October fourth. We serve 149, uh, cities and town in Connecticut, and every one of these communities suffered damage from Isaias, uh, much of it catastrophic. As you can see on the slide, we had nearly 22,000 damage locations that we had to address, and brought in an army of electric, uh, restoration and tree crews to restore power, all the while working on the restoration in a pandemic setting. The restoration process lasted nine days, meaning we completed our work one to two days faster than we had in the last two tropical storms that hit Connecticut, even though we had, uh, 30% to 35% more damage locations. And most importantly, we completed that work safely with no serious electrical contact and no COVID exposure among the enormous workforce we brought to Connecticut. Just, just a tremendous effort, uh, by all of our employees from across all parts of, of Eversource. At this time we estimate that deferred costs across all three states will total more than $275 million dollars, with the vast majority of that, um, incurred in Connecticut. That figure will be adjusted as the actual invoices are received. We're still, we're still actively pursuing, uh, invoices from hundreds of vendors that assisted us during the statewide, uh, restoration effort. Uh, where we were setting new poles or hanging miles of new wires or replacing hundreds of transforms, there related costs are to be capitalized. The ultimate recovery of storm costs and the evaluation on performance, uh, and safely and expeditiously restoring power to our customers is pending an ongoing review by the Connecticut Public Utilities Regulatory Authority, or PURA. That review is scheduled to be completed in late, uh, April of 2021. Sticking with our regulated business, I'll throw up the slide, uh, five and a review of this year's, uh, distribution rate reviews. This past Friday the Massachusetts Department of Public Utilities issued its decision in the NSTAR Gas Rate Review that we filed last year. It supports our continued investment in the NSTAR Gas system on behalf of our 300,000 customers. The decision allows NSTAR Gas to increase distribution revenues by $23 million dollars on an annualized basis. The BPU approved the <inaudible> of 9.9% and a capital structure with, uh, 54.77% equity. It also permits us, uh, to implement performance based rating for a 10-year term that, uh, which sounds, uh, uprating performance by NSTAR Gas will target annual base rate increases of inflation plus one point, uh, zero three percent. This is an earning sharing mechanism that would return 75% of the benefit to customers should we receive the ROE of 10.9%, and sharing mechanism on the down side if our ROE falls below 8.4%. Uh, also exciting is the decision also approves our first ever geothermal pilot program. Another longstanding rate proceeding involves Public Service of New Hampshire. In <inaudible> last month we and all the parties to the PSNH rate case filed a proposed settlement in the rate review that, that has been pending for nearly, um, you know, a year and a 